    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who commands an elite 80.75 score against Wandy Peralta. The combination of his #3 spot in the order and an 85% stolen base opportunity against a pitcher with slow delivery makes him our clear centerpiece. **Gleyber Torres** (67.52) and **Brendan Donovan** (66.36) also provide exceptional value; **Gleyber Torres** leverages a significant platoon advantage against Kay, while **Brendan Donovan** benefits from a favorable matchup against Wesneski. **Jung Hoo Lee** (65.65) rounds out our top-tier plays, as his ability to exploit the high xERA of Lorenzen, paired with a prime #4 spot, maximizes his potential to contribute in both R and SB categories.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical adjustments with **Cal Raleigh**, **Zack Gelof**, **Lawrence Butler**, **Bryce Harper**, and **Brendan Donovan** all awaiting final lineup confirmation. Of these, **Bryce Harper** (60.06) remains a must-start despite the tough matchup against Prielipp, bolstered by his impressive 0.389 xwOBA. The most difficult decision involved **Luis Arraez** (47.52), who narrowly missed the cut; while he offers a reliable floor, the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Jonathan Aranda** (62.19) due to the latter\u2019s superior platoon leverage against Bradish. **JJ Bleday** was also considered, but ultimately benched not only for efficiency concerns but also due to a 39% rain risk at his venue. \n\nWe are monitoring the **Masyn Winn** and **Lawrence Butler** spots closely. **Masyn Winn** holds a marginal score of 52.55; while we value his elite speed, the matchup against Boyd is less than ideal. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** is fighting a significant platoon penalty (-13%) against MacKenzie Gore. However, with the scarcity of stolen base threats in the current market, we are willing to absorb these lower scores to maintain our competitive edge in the SB category. Keep a close watch on the official lineups for **Zack Gelof** and **Cal Raleigh**, as any shift lower in the batting order could necessitate a re-evaluation of their daily efficiency scores.",
